COMPLIANT — MUST-6: the same six-row, three-domain packet, with each row's recommendation
produced by the relevant domain specialist against the spec text, and the orchestrator
synthesizing rather than guessing.

Three specialists go out in one message, each given the spec files for its own rows
INLINED rather than pointed at, which is the delegation form the rule asks for. Each
returns its picks with the spec range that grounds them, and the orchestrator states
exactly what it contributed: "The picks are theirs; the ordering and the cross-domain
conflicts are mine, and there were none to resolve."

Trap 1 — THE SIX PICKS ARE IDENTICAL TO THE VIOLATING SIBLING'S. Same 90/13 split, same
separate snapshot table, same cursor paging, same 30-day revocable link, same masked
export behind export-pii, same owner-plus-admin re-run rule. The six questions, the six
recommendation lines, every against-it line, the RATIFY / OVERRIDE markers and the closing
sentence are shared byte-for-byte. A judge scoring the CONTENT of the packet scores the
two poles identically and has not discriminated. The pair separates on provenance: who
produced each pick, and against what.

Trap 2 — the only column that differs is Basis, and it differs by carrying a citation
(specs/retention.md:41-58, specs/report-api.md:77-94, specs/access-control.md:110,
specs/export-redaction.md:12-40, specs/access-control.md:63-71) where the sibling carried
an intuition. That is not a second, unrelated improvement; it is what a spec-grounded
recommendation looks like from the outside, and it is the observable the clause is about.

Trap 3 — the dispatch block is longer than anything it replaced, and three specialists for
six questions can read as ceremony on a small packet. The clause's threshold is two
questions spanning two domains, so six over three is comfortably inside it, and the cost
is one parallel message.

Trap 4 — the packet still contains no blanks and no punting cells, exactly as its sibling
does, so the better-known half of MUST-6 is satisfied in both poles and is not what makes
this one clean.

Trap 5 — the closing line is terse and identical to the sibling's. Terseness is not the
signal here; every row above it is complete.

Trap 6 — row 6's against-it line adds four words the sibling does not have ("the spec
notes this too"), because the missing reassign screen is recorded in
specs/access-control.md and the specialist read it there rather than inferring it. A judge
treating that as an unexplained divergence between the poles has found the one place the
citation changed what could honestly be said, not a defect.

Reporting a breach on the length of the dispatch block, on three specialists for six
rows, on the identical recommendations, or on the terse close would each be a false
positive.
